            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A forensic or legal declaration by God whereby a sinner is declared righteous, acquitted of guilt, and granted right standing before God, typically through faith in Jesus Christ (historic Protestant view).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Romans 3:24-28\",\"Romans 4:5\",\"Romans 5:1\",\"Galatians 2:16\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A transformative process where an individual is intrinsically made righteous through the infusion of God's grace and active participation in good works (historic Roman Catholic view).\",\"basis\":[\"James 2:24\",\"Romans 5:19\",\"1 Corinthians 6:11\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"An ecclesiological and covenantal declaration that identifies who belongs to the covenant family of God, rather than merely a soteriological mechanism for dealing with individual guilt (New Perspective on Paul).\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 3:29-30\",\"Galatians 2:11-16\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A therapeutic process of healing and participation in the divine nature (Theosis or Deification), emphasizing organic union with Christ over forensic courtroom metaphors (Eastern Orthodox view).\",\"basis\":[\"2 Peter 1:4\",\"2 Corinthians 5:21\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While all major Christian traditions agree that justification results in a restored relationship with God, there is no single settled consensus on the theological mechanics. The historic divide between Protestant (forensic/declarative) and Roman Catholic (transformative/infused) interpretations remains foundational, alongside modern scholarly debates such as the New Perspective on Paul.\"}",
